1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,815
Tonight, every insect on Earth vanishes.

2
00:00:02,835 --> 00:00:04,748
Goodbye mosquitoes!

3
00:00:04,768 --> 00:00:05,876
Great news, right?

4
00:00:05,896 --> 00:00:06,590
Well...

5
00:00:06,610 --> 00:00:07,812
let's see what happens next.

6
00:00:08,813 --> 00:00:10,375
And that's a lot of insects.

7
00:00:10,395 --> 00:00:11,994
The Smithsonian estimates that

8
00:00:11,994 --> 00:00:13,691
about ten quintillion insects

9
00:00:13,691 --> 00:00:15,559
are alive at any one time.

10
00:00:15,579 --> 00:00:16,800
That's a one followed by

11
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:17,614
nineteen zeros.

12
00:00:20,614 --> 00:00:22,127
Scientists have named about a

13
00:00:22,127 --> 00:00:23,498
million insect species.

14
00:00:23,518 --> 00:00:24,502
But they estimate there are

15
00:00:24,502 --> 00:00:25,452
about five and a half

16
00:00:25,452 --> 00:00:26,422
million in total.

17
00:00:26,442 --> 00:00:27,522
So most of the insects

18
00:00:27,522 --> 00:00:29,096
that would vanish are still undiscovered.

19
00:00:30,096 --> 00:00:31,783
The first problem is flowers.

20
00:00:31,803 --> 00:00:32,933
Scientists estimate that

21
00:00:32,933 --> 00:00:34,350
nearly nine in ten flowering

22
00:00:34,350 --> 00:00:36,223
plant species rely on animals,

23
00:00:36,243 --> 00:00:37,313
mostly insects,

24
00:00:37,333 --> 00:00:38,463
to pollinate them.

25
00:00:38,483 --> 00:00:41,608
That's around 308,000 species.

26
00:00:41,628 --> 00:00:42,263
No visitors,

27
00:00:42,263 --> 00:00:42,819
no seeds,

28
00:00:42,819 --> 00:00:43,374
no fruit.

29
00:00:44,374 --> 00:00:45,557
What about our food?

30
00:00:45,577 --> 00:00:46,762
About three-quarters of the

31
00:00:46,762 --> 00:00:47,986
world's leading food crop

32
00:00:47,986 --> 00:00:49,506
types benefit at least partly

33
00:00:49,506 --> 00:00:50,891
from animal pollination.

34
00:00:50,911 --> 00:00:51,711
But by weight,

35
00:00:51,731 --> 00:00:52,992
those crops make up only

36
00:00:52,992 --> 00:00:54,026
about a third of what

37
00:00:54,026 --> 00:00:54,698
we grow.

38
00:00:54,718 --> 00:00:55,468
In money,

39
00:00:55,488 --> 00:00:56,477
scientists estimate

40
00:00:56,477 --> 00:00:58,231
pollinators help produce crops

41
00:00:58,231 --> 00:01:01,446
worth about 235 to 577

42
00:01:01,446 --> 00:01:03,028
billion US dollars,

43
00:01:03,048 --> 00:01:03,987
every single year.

44
00:01:05,337 --> 00:01:06,330
Why so little?

45
00:01:06,350 --> 00:01:07,403
Because staple grains like

46
00:01:07,403 --> 00:01:08,601
wheat, rice and maize are

47
00:01:08,601 --> 00:01:10,100
pollinated by the wind,

48
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:11,559
or pollinate themselves.

49
00:01:11,579 --> 00:01:12,707
So we'd still have bread

50
00:01:12,707 --> 00:01:13,385
and rice.

51
00:01:13,405 --> 00:01:14,350
But we'd lose much of

52
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:15,235
the variety:

53
00:01:15,255 --> 00:01:15,945
many fruits,

54
00:01:15,945 --> 00:01:17,411
vegetables, nuts and seeds.

55
00:01:18,411 --> 00:01:19,691
And brace yourself:

56
00:01:19,711 --> 00:01:20,633
chocolate.

57
00:01:20,653 --> 00:01:22,119
Cocoa flowers are pollinated

58
00:01:22,119 --> 00:01:23,606
mainly by tiny flies called

59
00:01:23,606 --> 00:01:25,517
midges, no bigger than a pinhead.

60
00:01:25,537 --> 00:01:26,231
No midges,

61
00:01:26,231 --> 00:01:27,273
no cocoa beans.

62
00:01:28,273 --> 00:01:29,613
Farmers know how much they

63
00:01:29,613 --> 00:01:30,429
need bees.

64
00:01:30,449 --> 00:01:32,336
Every spring, about two million honeybee

65
00:01:32,336 --> 00:01:33,414
hives are brought to

66
00:01:33,414 --> 00:01:35,389
California's almond orchards,

67
00:01:35,409 --> 00:01:36,381
many of them trucked in

68
00:01:36,381 --> 00:01:38,723
from across the United States.

69
00:01:38,743 --> 00:01:40,454
Could people do the bees' job?

70
00:01:40,474 --> 00:01:41,858
It has happened.

71
00:01:41,878 --> 00:01:43,388
In parts of Sichuan,

72
00:01:43,408 --> 00:01:44,839
in China, where heavy pesticide use

73
00:01:44,839 --> 00:01:46,348
helped wipe out local bees,

74
00:01:46,368 --> 00:01:47,959
farmers have pollinated apple

75
00:01:47,959 --> 00:01:49,616
and pear blossoms by hand.

76
00:01:49,636 --> 00:01:51,066
Flower, by flower,

77
00:01:51,086 --> 00:01:52,106
by flower.

78
00:01:52,126 --> 00:01:53,416
In one county there,

79
00:01:53,436 --> 00:01:54,596
around 2001,

80
00:01:54,616 --> 00:01:56,019
nearly all the apple trees

81
00:01:56,019 --> 00:01:58,153
were pollinated by people.

82
00:01:58,173 --> 00:01:59,518
Slow, tiring work that bees

83
00:01:59,518 --> 00:02:00,218
do for free.

84
00:02:01,219 --> 00:02:03,300
Next, the animals that eat insects.

85
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:04,819
Scientists estimate that the

86
00:02:04,819 --> 00:02:06,294
world's insect-eating birds

87
00:02:06,294 --> 00:02:08,543
eat about 400 to 500

88
00:02:08,543 --> 00:02:10,115
million tonnes of insects and

89
00:02:10,115 --> 00:02:11,541
other small creepy-crawlies

90
00:02:11,541 --> 00:02:12,739
every year.

91
00:02:12,759 --> 00:02:14,108
Researchers compared it to the

92
00:02:14,108 --> 00:02:15,334
energy a city the size

93
00:02:15,334 --> 00:02:16,558
of New York uses in

94
00:02:16,558 --> 00:02:17,235
a year.

95
00:02:17,255 --> 00:02:18,485
Without insects,

96
00:02:18,505 --> 00:02:19,275
many birds,

97
00:02:19,295 --> 00:02:20,607
frogs and fish would go hungry.

98
00:02:21,607 --> 00:02:23,230
And then, the mess.

99
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:24,620
Insects are part of nature's

100
00:02:24,620 --> 00:02:25,555
clean-up crew.

101
00:02:25,575 --> 00:02:26,255
Dung beetles,

102
00:02:26,275 --> 00:02:27,095
for example,

103
00:02:27,115 --> 00:02:28,469
break up animal dung and

104
00:02:28,469 --> 00:02:29,602
bury it back into the

105
00:02:29,602 --> 00:02:31,597
soil, where it feeds the ground.

106
00:02:31,617 --> 00:02:32,122
Without them,

107
00:02:32,122 --> 00:02:32,947
dung can sit on the

108
00:02:32,947 --> 00:02:34,187
surface, rot slowly,

109
00:02:34,207 --> 00:02:36,623
and become a home for flies.

110
00:02:36,643 --> 00:02:37,851
Australia learned this the

111
00:02:37,851 --> 00:02:38,654
hard way.

112
00:02:38,674 --> 00:02:39,742
Its native dung beetles

113
00:02:39,742 --> 00:02:40,715
evolved with kangaroo

114
00:02:40,715 --> 00:02:42,093
droppings, and couldn't cope

115
00:02:42,093 --> 00:02:43,220
with big cow pats.

116
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:45,710
So from the late 1960s,

117
00:02:45,730 --> 00:02:47,477
scientists at CSIRO brought in

118
00:02:47,477 --> 00:02:49,052
dozens of dung beetle species

119
00:02:49,052 --> 00:02:50,410
from Africa and Europe to

120
00:02:50,410 --> 00:02:51,122
bury the dung.

121
00:02:52,472 --> 00:02:54,582
For people, it's not instant famine,

122
00:02:54,602 --> 00:02:55,695
but it's serious.

123
00:02:55,715 --> 00:02:57,438
One modelling study estimated

124
00:02:57,438 --> 00:02:58,743
that losing all animal

125
00:02:58,743 --> 00:03:00,187
pollinators could lead to

126
00:03:00,187 --> 00:03:02,353
about 1.4 million extra deaths

127
00:03:02,353 --> 00:03:02,915
a year,

128
00:03:02,935 --> 00:03:03,675
from heart disease,

129
00:03:03,695 --> 00:03:05,289
strokes, some cancers and

130
00:03:05,289 --> 00:03:06,665
missing nutrients,

131
00:03:06,685 --> 00:03:07,818
because healthy fruits and

132
00:03:07,818 --> 00:03:09,234
vegetables would become scarce.

133
00:03:10,584 --> 00:03:11,804
Here's the twist:

134
00:03:11,824 --> 00:03:13,107
insects don't need to vanish

135
00:03:13,107 --> 00:03:14,177
overnight to matter.

136
00:03:14,197 --> 00:03:15,847
In German nature reserves,

137
00:03:15,867 --> 00:03:17,217
the weight of flying insects

138
00:03:17,217 --> 00:03:18,481
caught in traps fell by

139
00:03:18,481 --> 00:03:19,744
about three-quarters over

140
00:03:19,744 --> 00:03:21,117
twenty-seven years,

141
00:03:21,137 --> 00:03:23,252
measured at sixty-three sites.

142
00:03:23,272 --> 00:03:23,882
In mid-summer,

143
00:03:23,902 --> 00:03:25,892
the drop was even bigger:

144
00:03:25,912 --> 00:03:27,812
about eighty-two percent.

145
00:03:27,832 --> 00:03:28,762
That's one region,

146
00:03:28,782 --> 00:03:29,642
not the whole world,

147
00:03:29,662 --> 00:03:31,274
but scientists are watching closely.

148
00:03:32,274 --> 00:03:34,051
So, a world without insects?

149
00:03:34,071 --> 00:03:35,181
Fewer flowers,

150
00:03:35,201 --> 00:03:35,781
hungrier birds,

151
00:03:35,801 --> 00:03:36,361
dung everywhere,

152
00:03:36,381 --> 00:03:38,056
and much less fruit on

153
00:03:38,056 --> 00:03:39,040
your plate.

154
00:03:39,060 --> 00:03:40,387
Next time you see a

155
00:03:40,387 --> 00:03:42,084
bee, maybe say thanks.

156
00:03:42,104 --> 00:03:43,797
Even Newton agrees.

157
00:03:43,817 --> 00:03:45,265
He's a cactus.

158
00:03:45,285 --> 00:03:46,190
He knows how hard it

159
00:03:46,190 --> 00:03:47,292
is to find a pollinator

160
00:03:47,292 --> 00:03:47,834
in the desert.

161
00:03:49,234 --> 00:03:50,394
Which insect would you miss

162
00:03:50,394 --> 00:03:51,130
the most?

163
00:03:51,150 --> 00:03:52,378
Tell me in the comments.

164
00:03:52,398 --> 00:03:53,608
Next time:

165
00:03:53,628 --> 00:03:54,264
what if you could see

166
00:03:54,264 --> 00:03:54,948
like a bee,

167
00:03:54,968 --> 00:03:56,734
with colours we can't even imagine?

168
00:03:56,754 --> 00:03:58,166
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
